@title CSS and JS in Extensions
@group developer

Explains how to add and use CSS and JS files in Phorge extension.

= Overview =

Phorge extensions use the same system as Phorge itself, Celerity,
to manage static resources. By and large, this is similar to how Phorge
handles its static resources.

= Adding Celerity Support To an Extension =

To add Celerity support to an extension, first create a directory to store the
CSS and JS in. Phorge uses `rsrc` in `webroot`. This is misleading, because
the directory doesn't have to be web-accessible. Most extensions already have
a `resources` directory. Creating a `rsrc` directory there is the informal
convention and will make subclassing `CelerityExtensionResources` easier.

Next, create a directory where Celerity should store its map and package list.
Phorge uses `celerity` under `resources`, and `CelerityExtensionResources`
assumes this as well.

Then, create a sub class of `CelerityExtensionResources`. This class will
automatically provide access to Phorge's Celerity resources, which is required
in order to use Javelin classes and behaviors.

Finally, after you've added some CSS or Javascript, run Phorge's
`bin/celerity map` to have Celerity fill the map with the resources you have
created.

= Updating the Celerity Map =

Just like Phorge, you run `bin/celerity map` to update the Celerity map.
Phorge will automatically update all Celerity maps that it knows of.
This includes extensions that are loaded through `load-libraries`.

= Including a File =

This is the same as Phorge does it, except that the call to
@{function:require_celerity_resource} must have the name of the extension
specified as the second argument:

  require_celerity_resource('extension-style-css', 'extension-name');
